 things like sitting on a chair I cannot  go from a standing to a seating position  with grace due to the de standards of  design heights of chairs I have to crawl  on my hands and knees just to get on top  this well it's also being conscious that  it might tip over at any stage but was  design impacts upon me whether it's a  chair a bathroom a coffee shop or  clothes I rely on and benefit from the  kindness of strangers but not everybody  is so nice I'm reminded that I'm a  little person when a stranger points  stairs  laughs calls me a name or takes a  photograph of me this happens almost  every day with the rise of social media  has given me an opportunity and a  platform to have a voice as a blogger  and as an activist but it has also made  me nervous that I might become a meme or  a viral sensation or without my consent  so let's take a moment  right now to make something very clear  the word midges is a slur it evolved  from PT Barnum's era of circuses and  freak shows society has evolved so  should our vocabulary language is a  powerful tool it does not just name our  society it shapes it I am incredibly  proud to be a little person to have  inherited the condition of  achondroplasia but I am most proud to be  sinead achondroplasia is the most common  form of dwarfism achondroplasia  translates as without cartilage  formation I have short limbs and  achondroplastic facial features my  forehead and my nose my arms do not  straighten fully but I can lick my elbow  I'm not showing you that one  achondroplasia occurs in approximately  one in every twenty thousand births 80%  of little people are wanted to average  highs parents that means that anybody in  this room could have a child with  achondroplasia yes I inherited my  condition from my dad I'd like to show  you a photo of my family my mother is  average height my father is a little  person and I am the eldest of five  children I have three sisters and one  brother they are all average height I am  incredibly fortunate to have been born  into a family that cultivated my  curiosity and my tenacity that protected  me from the unkindness and ignorance of  strangers and that armed me with the  resilience creativity and confidence  that I needed to survive and manipulate  the physical environment and society if  I was to pinpoint any reason why I am  successful is because I was and I am a  loved child now I love trials with a lot  of sass and sarcasm but I love childs  nonetheless in giving you an insight  into  Who I am today I wanted to offer you a  new perspective I wanted to challenge  the idea that design has brought a tool  to create function and beauty design  greatly impacts upon people's lives all  lives design is a way in which we can  feel included in the world but it is  also a way in which we can uphold a  person's dignity and their human rights  design can also inflict vulnerability on  a group whose needs aren't considered so  today I wondered perceptions challenged  who are we not designing for how can we  amplify their voices and their  experiences what is the next step design  is an enormous privilege but it is a  bigger responsibility I want you to open  your eyes thank you so much  [Applause]
 

